  "query_preview": "..Water vapor imagery shows a fairly anomalous \nupper level low centered over central California with ridging \nover the northern Great Plains and Midwest. Models keep this low\nover the same general area through tonight, then open it up \nFriday as it moves eastward through the Great Basin. This will \nkeep temperatures 6-10 degrees below normal through Saturday for\nmost of the area. Far western Pima County will be 10-14 degrees\nbelow normal today and Friday thanks to its proximity to the low\nand surface moisture from the Gulf of California. Winds will \nremain breezy today and tomorrow with Friday likely seeing \nslightly stronger winds than today as the southern periphery of \nthe low moves through Arizona. This will lead to elevated fire \nweather conditions both days with some areas along the AZ/NM \nborder seeing brief and localized critical fire weather \nconditions Friday afternoon.\n \nAfter Saturday ensembles keep the main storm track to the north\nwith high pressure slowly starting to build in over the southern\nPlains. This is a slight change over previous runs which had the\nhigh further west close to the Four Corners. A cutoff low is\nalso expected to form over Baja by Tuesday or Wednesday. With \nheights increasing, so will temperatures. The warmup begins \nSunday with temperatures 0-4 degrees above normal through the\nwork week. With the high to the east and weak cutoff low to the\nsouthwest Arizona will be under southerly mid-level flow which \nwill advect some mid-level moisture into southeast Arizona. \nHowever with the high further east instead of over the more\nfavorable Four Corners, moisture looks to be a little more \nlimited than in previous runs. But enough will be in place for \nthunderstorms chances Tuesday into the later part of the week. \nRight now the best chances look to be Wednesday mainly east of \nTucson where PoPs are 20-50 percent. Best chances will likely be\nalong the AZ/NM border. Confidence is low at this time since \nthere is lots of uncertainty with the synoptic situation along \nwith how far west the moisture will push.",
